//! lotus/stereo_insert_silence.rye — ALES123: stereo_insert_silence — opening a silent gap carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep. ALES109 named `insert_silence` — open
//! `count` samples of silence at position `at`, shifting the tail right so the clip GROWS by exactly `count` and every existing sample is KEPT — for a mono Clip. Yet a Lotus master is a
//! StereoClip (ALES10), two Clips heard together whose defining invariant is that LEFT and RIGHT hold the SAME length so the two channels stay aligned in time. This rung carries
//! insert_silence into stereo the way ALES120 carried duplicate and ALES122 carried silence_span: run ALES109's proven mono `insert_silence` on each channel with the SAME `at` and
//! `count`, so the gap opens identically in both and the stereo image stays balanced. It is the growth-twin of ALES122's stereo_silence_span — where silence_span writes zeros over an
//! existing span and the length HOLDS, insert_silence opens new zero seats and the length GROWS: silence written in place versus silence made room for.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: stereo_insert_silence(sc, at, count) opens `count` silent samples at `at` in both channels, reusing ALES109's mono `insert_silence` per channel over its public
//! API. BOTH channels are validated BEFORE either is mutated — because mono insert_silence can fault only with BadRange (an insertion point past the samples) or ClipFull (a grow past the
//! fixed buffer) and both channels enter equal-length over the same fixed buffer, the two checks are made once against the shared length up front, so once they pass each mono
//! insert_silence is pre-validated to succeed and a refusal never desynchronises the channels. It reuses ALES2's EditError whole and invents none of its own.
//!
//! The crux — four laws. THE STEREO INSERT LAW: the left channel equals mono insert_silence(left, at, count) and the right equals mono insert_silence(right, at, count), each byte for
//! byte, both grown by count, the opened gap all zero and every existing sample kept. THE BALANCE / INVARIANT LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit, both grown by exactly count, the
//! stereo image staying aligned. THE INVERSE LAW: stereo_insert_silence(at, count) then ALES117's stereo_cut(at, count) returns both channels to the original byte for byte — insert_silence
//! is the EXACT inverse of cut, the lossless round-trip lifted from mono ALES109 into stereo. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: any refusal leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and
//! still balanced; count 0 the identity on both, at = len appending silence at the tail, a bad insertion point BadRange, a grow past the buffer ClipFull.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES123.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-022429_fill-ales123-lotus-stereo-insert-silence.md
//! Stands on: lotus/insert_silence.rye (ALES109) — the mono gap-opening gesture run once per channel; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip (two mono Clips, left/right) and its balance
//! invariant; lotus/stereo_cut.rye (ALES117) — the stereo span-remove imported by the selftest to prove the inverse law; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, its max_clip bound, and the
//! EditError reused whole; lotus/meter.rye (ALES13) — the peak/RMS meter, used only by the witness to read the result.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It opens a gap in each channel through the proven mono
//! insert_silence and fills it with SILENCE (zero) — the one thing it writes that was not already there, and silence is the honest content of a gap a keeper opened on purpose; it invents
//! no non-zero sample, drops nothing, and reads no byte past either channel's len. No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_insert_silence.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const insert_silence = @import("insert_silence.rye");
const stereo_cut = @import("stereo_cut.rye");
const meter = @import("meter.rye");

pub const stereo_insert_silence_version = "20260815.022429";

/// stereo_insert_silence forwards ALES2's OWN faults reused by name — BadRange (an insertion point past the samples) and ClipFull (a grow past the fixed buffer) — and invents none of its own.
pub const StereoInsertSilenceError = timeline.EditError;

/// Open `count` samples of silence at position `at` in both channels in lockstep, shifting each tail right so the clip grows by exactly `count`, the stereo image staying balanced. Both
/// channels are validated before either is mutated: because mono insert_silence can fault only with BadRange or ClipFull and the channels enter equal-length over the same fixed buffer, the
/// two checks are made once against the shared length up front, so a refusal never leaves one channel grown and the other not. Reuses ALES109's mono insert_silence per channel.
pub fn stereo_insert_silence(sc: *pan.StereoClip, at: u32, count: u32) StereoInsertSilenceError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // invariant: the insertion point lies within the (shared) current samples, refused by name before any write — the same guard ALES109's insert_silence keeps, run once against the
    //            common length so a refusal lands before either channel mutates.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (at > len) return error.BadRange;

    // invariant: the grow of `count` samples fits within the fixed buffer for both channels alike (equal length, equal max_clip), refused ClipFull before any write, checked without
    //            underflow — count against the free seats that remain.
    if (count > timeline.max_clip - len) return error.ClipFull;

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels, so each mono insert_silence is pre-validated to succeed — no refusal can land between the two channel edits.
    try insert_silence.insert_silence(&sc.left, at, count);
    try insert_silence.insert_silence(&sc.right, at, count);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced — each grew by the same opened span, so the stereo image stays aligned.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO INSERT LAW — each channel equals ALES109's mono insert_silence on that channel byte for byte, both grown by count, the gap all zero; THE BALANCE / INVARIANT LAW
//     — left.len == right.len after, both grown by count; THE INVERSE LAW — insert_silence then ALES117's stereo_cut(at, count) returns both channels; THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW — a
//     refusal leaves both channels untouched, count 0 the identity, at = len appends at the tail, a grow past the buffer ClipFull ----------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO INSERT LAW — each channel equals ALES109's mono insert_silence on that channel, proven side by side over one anchor with different per-channel samples.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 }, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        try stereo_insert_silence(&sc, 2, 2); // open two silent seats at index 2 in both channels

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 });
        try insert_silence.insert_silence(&ml, 2, 2);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41, 51 });
        try insert_silence.insert_silence(&mr, 2, 2);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftChannelWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightChannelWrong;
        // Concretely: {10,20,0,0,30,40,50} on the left, {11,21,0,0,31,41,51} on the right; both grown by 2, the gap all zero, every existing sample kept.
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10, 20, 0, 0, 30, 40, 50 })) return error.LeftSpanWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 11, 21, 0, 0, 31, 41, 51 })) return error.RightSpanWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE BALANCE / INVARIANT LAW — the channels stay equal length and each grows by exactly count; the stereo image stays aligned. Proven at the head and appending at the tail.
    {
        var head = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&head, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 6, 7, 8 });
        try stereo_insert_silence(&head, 0, 2); // open at the head
        if (head.left.len != head.right.len) return error.HeadUnbalanced;
        if (head.left.len != 5) return error.HeadLength;
        if (!eq(head.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 1, 2, 3 })) return error.HeadLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(head.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 6, 7, 8 })) return error.HeadRightWrong;

        var tail = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&tail, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 6, 7, 8 });
        try stereo_insert_silence(&tail, 3, 2); // append silence at the tail (at == len)
        if (tail.left.len != tail.right.len) return error.TailUnbalanced;
        if (!eq(tail.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3, 0, 0 })) return error.TailLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(tail.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 6, 7, 8, 0, 0 })) return error.TailRightWrong;
    }

    // 3. THE INVERSE LAW — stereo_insert_silence(at, count) then ALES117's stereo_cut(at, count) returns both channels to the original byte for byte: open a gap, then remove exactly it.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        const l0 = [_]i16{ 10, 20, 30, 40 };
        const r0 = [_]i16{ 11, 21, 31, 41 };
        try load_stereo(&sc, &l0, &r0);
        try stereo_insert_silence(&sc, 1, 2); // {10,0,0,20,30,40} both channels
        try stereo_cut.stereo_cut(&sc, 1, 2); // remove the opened gap at at = 1
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &l0)) return error.InverseLeftWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &r0)) return error.InverseRightWrong;
    }

    // 4a. THE ATOMICITY LAW — a refusal leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced, proven for an insertion point past the samples and a grow past the fixed buffer.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 }, &[_]i16{ 4, 5, 6 });

        if (stereo_insert_silence(&sc, 4, 1)) |_| return error.AtNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongError;
        }
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.RefusedWroteLeft;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 4, 5, 6 })) return error.RefusedWroteRight;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.RefusedUnbalanced;

        // A grow past the fixed buffer refuses ClipFull before any write — fill both channels to their bound, then an insert that would grow past it must refuse, both untouched.
        var full = pan.StereoClip{};
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < timeline.max_clip) : (i += 1) {
            try timeline.splice(&full.left, i, &[_]i16{1});
            try timeline.splice(&full.right, i, &[_]i16{2});
        }
        const full_len = full.left.len;
        if (stereo_insert_silence(&full, 0, 1)) |_| return error.FullNotRefused else |e| {
            if (e != error.ClipFull) return error.WrongFullError;
        }
        if (full.left.len != full_len) return error.RefusedGrewLeft;
        if (full.right.len != full_len) return error.RefusedGrewRight;
        if (full.left.len != full.right.len) return error.RefusedFullUnbalanced;
    }

    // 4b. THE DEGENERATE LAW — count 0 the identity on both channels.
    {
        var zero = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&zero, &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 }, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 });
        try stereo_insert_silence(&zero, 1, 0); // open nothing — the identity
        if (!eq(zero.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, 8, 9 })) return error.ZeroLeftMoved;
        if (!eq(zero.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 })) return error.ZeroRightMoved;
    }

    _ = meter;

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-insert-silence: STEREO_INSERT_SILENCE — opening a silent gap carried into stereo, both channels in lockstep. Open count samples of silence at at in both " ++
        "channels, reusing ALES109's mono insert_silence per channel; a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10) whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length, so " ++
        "opening a gap for a downbeat lands it in both speakers at once. It is the growth-twin of ALES122's stereo_silence_span — silence_span writes zeros over an existing span and the " ++
        "length holds, insert_silence opens new zero seats and the length grows: silence written in place versus silence made room for. BOTH channels are validated before either is " ++
        "mutated — because mono insert_silence can fault only with BadRange or ClipFull and the channels enter equal-length over the same fixed buffer, the two checks are made once " ++
        "against the shared length up front, so a refusal never desynchronises the channels. The crux is four laws. THE STEREO INSERT LAW: each channel equals ALES109's mono " ++
        "insert_silence on that channel byte for byte, both grown by count, the gap all zero and every existing sample kept. THE BALANCE / INVARIANT LAW: left.len == right.len after the " ++
        "edit, both grown by count, the stereo image staying aligned. THE INVERSE LAW: insert_silence(at, count) then ALES117's stereo_cut(at, count) returns both channels to the " ++
        "original. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: any refusal leaves both channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced, count 0 the identity on both, at = len appending at the " ++
        "tail, a grow past the buffer ClipFull. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips grown through the proven mono insert_silence, reading no byte past either channel's len; no real " ++
        "sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
